+### Bounded row-reduction frontend v3
+
+`RowReductionV3` is private compiler analysis, not a new public operator,
+compiler facade, or wire type. It emits entry `pypto_row_reduction_v3` and
+metadata schema 3. It accepts one Opaque function with exactly one exact `In`
+parameter, one direct assignment carrying registered `tensor.row_sum` or
+`tensor.row_max`, and one direct return of that assignment. The call has one
+operand and empty attributes and keyword arguments.
+
+The input is a plain, fully static, contiguous row-major BF16 or FP32 tensor of
+rank 1 through 32, with no MemRef or TensorView. The result preserves rank and
+HIR dtype and has the same shape except that the last extent is one. TensorIR
+uses a built-in last-axis `reduce` with mode `add` or `max`. BF16 never reaches a
+BF16-typed reduction: canonical source converts the input BF16 tensor to FP32,
+reduces in FP32, then converts the FP32 result back to BF16.
+For rank greater than two, canonical source reshapes the compute tensor to
+`[rows, shape[-1]]`, reduces it to `[rows, 1]`, then reshapes back to the HIR
+result shape. This is what makes the single row tile and flattened row-grid law
+exact rather than producer-dependent.
+
+The ABI contains exactly ordered `Input0` and `Result0` tensor descriptors, one
+read-only input plus a `nil` result alias, two pointer arguments, static grid
+policy selected by descriptor zero, zero workspace, and the shared fixed launch
+and loader contracts. Rank-one descriptors have implicit strides; higher ranks
+carry exact logical dense strides for the input and reduced result independently.
+
+V3 does not accept pointwise prefixes, multiple reductions, arbitrary-axis or
+rank-dropping reductions, dynamic extents, valid-shape/view metadata, mutation,
+CUDA Graph, runtime launch, numerical correctness, or performance. Those need
+separately versioned frontend and real-SM120 acceptance gates. The V1 and V2
+canonical sources, projection bytes, identities, and producer behavior remain
+unchanged.
+
